  "worked_solution": "In order to find the value of $B,$ we first need to find the period of the function.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that function makes $3$ complete cycles on the interval $[0,2\\pi ].$ So, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254788/assets/c17d8cbd806e89fbb2b1157d7096b5092949c37c7da12c9f55d4269a313458bf.png)\n\nWe know that for a function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx),$ the horizontal scaling factor is $\\frac{1}{B},$ and the period is multiplied by the scaling factor:\n\n$$\n\\text{period}=2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B}\n$$\n\nFor our particular function, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\pi }{3} & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B} \\\\ \\frac{2\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{B} \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
